Original commit body from 139e4d0752531af619b218e467b66652a49ac9bd:

scala.io.StdIn.readLine() written inline, with no import, emitted
scala:external:0-0:readLine. readLine is in scala.yaml's
ambiguous_names, so the no-module gate withheld it and the stdin
reader's ipc_recv row was present and unreachable, while the imported
forms reached it. The receiver branch took the receiver from a sibling
identifier of the method; an inline path parses as nested
field_expression nodes, so there was none and the path was dropped. The
same shape lost scala.sys.process.Process(cmd) and
java.nio.file.Files.readAllBytes(p).

A receiver that is a chain of identifiers is now read as a path and, as
WI-pokam already rules for a type written inline, taken as the module
when its root is scala / java / javax and the file does not bind that
name. A lowercase segment after a capitalised one names a member value,
not a module (scala.Console.err, measured on sbt), and keeps the
sentinel; so do a chain on a local value and a third-party root. A
capitalised callee on an all-lowercase path is a companion apply and
gets the imported form's slot, scala.sys.process.Process.

Measured, pinned dev vs branch, io-boundaries: sbt gains three fs_read
chains (Files.exists, Files.readAllBytes, Source.fromInputStream), lila
one (Source.fromFile); nothing lost on either; the remaining gains are
named JDK/Scala calls in external_potential (including scala.xml.XML.save,
an uncatalogued file write).

Residual filed: kotlin emits no edge at all for the same inline shape
(INV-dupol). Java already handles it.

Addresses INV-vokut.

